+# v1.1 Internal Consistency Pass
+
+**Run 2026-09-02, after all remediation edits and before packaging.**
+
+Fifteen named checks. Each states what was checked, how, what was found, and what was done. **Six found real defects introduced or left by the remediation itself**, which is the point of running it — a remediation that introduces its own contradictions has traded one class of defect for another.
+
+Verdict scale: **CLEAN** · **DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED** · **RESIDUAL ACCEPTED**.
+
+---
+
+## C1 — No document still describes the model as composing the effect
+
+**Method.** `grep -rn 'propose_effect'` across all deliverables, control documents, diagrams and README.
+
+**Found.** Nine live occurrences after the primary edits: `24 §16`, `29 §2`, `30 §6.3`, `33 §5`, `38 §3`, `README`, and three mermaid sources (`23-02.mmd`, `33-01.mmd`, `33-02.mmd`).
+
+**Fixed.** All nine replaced with `propose_intent`. `33-02.mmd` additionally gained the canonicalisation step so the diagram matches `33 §3.2`'s sequence. Seven remaining occurrences are historical references inside v1.1 change-record tables and ADR-006's *"the rename is not cosmetic"* passage, which are correct and must stay.
+
+**Verdict: DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED.**
+
+---
+
+## C2 — No policy sketch reads a model-authored parameter
+
+**Method.** `grep -rn 'context\.parameters'`.
+
+**Found.** Eight occurrences, all inside v1.1 change records and the `26 §8` preamble explaining why the sketches changed. Zero live policy sketches read it.
+
+**Verdict: CLEAN.**
+
+---
+
+## C3 — Authorised loss is never referred to as a single quantity
+
+**Method.** `grep` for bare `MAL(` outside `MAL_total`, `MAL_monetary` and historical citations.
+
+**Found.** Zero live instances. `26 §10`, `30 §3` V2, `29 §9`, `33 §5`, `35 §2`, `37 §5` and `51` all use the four-quantity form. `MAL_monetary` is consistently identified as the signed figure and `MAL_total` as the displayed one.
+
+**Verdict: CLEAN.**
+
+---
+
+## C4 — The CEO is never described as memoryless
+
+**Method.** `grep -rn 'memoryless'`.
+
+**Found.** Two live instances in diagram node labels — `diagrams/33-01.mmd` and the same diagram embedded in `33 §2`. Both said *"CEO function — scheduled, memoryless."* Every prose instance was already a corrected historical reference.
+
+**Fixed.** Both relabelled *"CEO function — DEFERRED · scheduled; no private or ungoverned memory."* The deferral marker was added because the diagram otherwise implies a component the MVP builds.
+
+**Verdict: DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED.** Worth noting that both instances were in diagrams, which is where drift hides — a prose sweep would have missed them.
+
+---
+
+## C5 — Every invariant citation resolves to the registry
+
+**Method.** Regex-extracted every `I<n>` token from all deliverables and control documents; compared the set against the registry's row identifiers.
+
+**Found.** Zero cited identifiers absent from the registry, after the primary edits corrected forty-one wrong citations across `28`, `33`, `34`, `35`, `36` and `37`. One residual: `33 §11` still referred to *"making `I1`–`I16` deterministic"*, a range that no longer describes the set.
+
+**Fixed.** Replaced with a pointer to the registry and a note that v1.0's range was a numbering four documents disagreed about.
+
+**Verdict: DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED.**
+
+---
+
+## C6 — Registry counts match the registry
+
+**Method.** Counted rows, distinct identifiers, audit-owned rows, and the MVP set implied by §2's property table; compared against every stated count in the registry and in citing documents.
+
+**Found.** Three arithmetic defects, all introduced by the remediation.
+
+- The registry claimed **54 identifiers**; it has **52**.
+- It claimed **24 in the MVP set**; §2's property table listed **33**, and §2's exclusion list accounted for **13** of 52 — leaving **six identifiers (I9, I16, I27, I35, I38, I50) in neither list.**
+- It claimed **18 audit-plane-owned**; the ownership column marks **20**.
+
+**Fixed.** The six unaccounted identifiers were assigned to the properties they prove (I9 → property 5, I16/I35/I50 → property 2, I27 → property 7, I38 → property 8), making the MVP set **39**, which reconciles exactly with 52 total minus 13 deferred. Counts corrected to **52 / 39 / 20** in the registry and propagated to `30 §6.1`, `33 §11`, `36 §11`, `37 §3`, `README` and the changelog. `30 §6.1`'s audit-owned enumeration was aligned member-for-member with the registry's ownership column.
+
+**Verdict: DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED.** This was the most consequential finding of the pass, and it is exactly the failure mode `47` criticised in v1.0 — **a count asserted in prose that nobody recomputed from the thing it counts.** The registry is now the only place the numbers live, and they were derived from it rather than copied into it.
+
+---
+
+## C7 — Every materially affected artifact carries a version header and a change record
+
+**Method.** Read line 3 of each deliverable; `grep -L 'v1.1 change record'`.
+
+**Found.** `32-architecture-options.md` was still at **v1.0** with no change record — and it is materially affected, because R18's finding that Temporal destroys the single-transaction property is a statement about the Option A/Option B boundary this document defines.
+
+**Fixed.** `32` promoted to v1.1 with a change record recording the boundary restatement, plus a reading note that *"stays in Postgres"* rather than *"modular monolith"* is Option A's load-bearing property. No option was re-scored.
+
+`48`–`51` correctly carry *"New in v1.1"* rather than a change record.
+
+**Verdict: DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED.**
+
+---
+
+## C8 — No document depends on the removed credential broker
+
+**Method.** `grep -rn 'CredentialBroker\|credential broker'`.
+
+**Found.** One live dependency: `29`'s threat-summary table listed *"credential broker"* among the controls for threat rows 1–7 — the authority-and-money block.
+
+**Fixed.** Replaced with the Effect Canonicaliser and the controls that actually apply under option A: per-adapter secrets, per-adapter isolation, revocation-not-pause, and I15's empirical probe. Every other occurrence is a removal note or an ADR-024 forward reference.
+
+**Verdict: DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED.**
+
+---
+
+## C9 — Langfuse is consistently dropped
+
+**Method.** `grep -rln 'Langfuse'` and inspection of each occurrence.
+
+**Found.** Ten documents mention it. All ten are either removal records (`23 §10`, `31 §10`, `34 ADR-012`, `37 §4`, `48 §2`), egress-inventory findings (`29 §4.1`, `49 §3.13`), or historical retention/licence citations that remain factually correct (`22 §B`, `30 §2`). No document specifies wiring it.
+
+**Verdict: CLEAN.**
+
+---
+
+## C10 — No document claims a sixteen-invariant set or a two-currency ledger
+
+**Method.** `grep` for `sixteen invariants`, `sixteen continuous`, `two currencies`, `two budget currencies`.
+
+**Found.** Zero live instances. The one surviving `sixteen` is `36 §15`'s reference to sixteen **control-artifact classes**, which is `50`'s count and correct.
+
+**Verdict: CLEAN.**
+
+---
+
+## C11 — The symcc property set is consistently P1–P7
+
+**Method.** `grep -rn 'P1–P6\|P1-P6'`.
+
+**Found.** Four live instances: `33 §4`, `34 ADR-005` (twice), `37 §7`.
+
+**Fixed.** All updated to the nine-property set (P1, P2, P3, P4, P4a, P5, P5a, P6, P7). ADR-005 additionally gained the deferral amendment and a note that deferring the gate defers its own reconsideration trigger with it. `37 §7`'s falsification condition now records that the interim substitute is `26 §11.2`'s hand proof and that it is weaker.
+
+**Verdict: DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED.**
+
+---
+
+## C12 — The audit-unreachable behaviour is specified once
+
+**Method.** Read every passage describing audit-store or audit-mirror unavailability across `24 §3` K11, `25`, `30 §5`, `33`, `35 §12.1`, `36 §6` and `38`.
+
+**Found.** All seven now specify **halt by recoverability class** per `30 §5.1`, and each references that section rather than restating the table. The one remaining bare *"halt effects"* phrase, in `38 §4`, is inside the EM15↔EM16 conflict record and correctly describes the **strict** reading being rejected.
+
+**Verdict: CLEAN.** This is the check that would have caught v1.0's AUDA-01 defect, and it is the reason it is in the list.
+
+---
+
+## C13 — T-U1 is nowhere presented as an MVP capability
+
+**Method.** `grep -rn 'T-U1'` across all deliverables, with each occurrence inspected for whether it implies the tier is built.
+
+**Found.** All occurrences either state the deferral, specify the twelve conditions it must satisfy before being built, or are historical corrections. `23 §9.2`, `26 §9.3`, `26 §9.6`, `33 §5`, `35 §10`, `36 §8.4`, `37 S4` and ADR-015 agree.
+
+**Verdict: CLEAN.**
+
+---
+
+## C14 — The four new artifacts are reachable from the documents that depend on them
+
+**Method.** For each of `48`–`51`, counted the documents citing it.
+
+**Found.** `48` cited in 15 documents, `49` in 16, `50` in 8, `51` in 12. Each is referenced from the deliverable whose claim it supports: `48` from `33 §1` and `29 §4`; `49` from `26 §10.4`, `29 §14` and `33 §11`; `50` from `26 §9`, `28 §4` and `30 §4`; `51` from `26 §10`, `36 §12` and `37 §5`. All four appear in the README manifest and the changelog.
+
+**Verdict: CLEAN.**
+
+---
+
+## C15 — Structural integrity
+
+**Method.** Balanced-fence check on every markdown file; single-column table-row scan; search for edit artifacts (`TODO`, `FIXME`, conflict markers); duplicate-heading scan per file.
+
+**Found.** No unbalanced code fences, no malformed table rows, no edit artifacts, no duplicate second-level headings. Section numbering is monotonic in every deliverable, including the four with sections fully replaced (`26 §8`–`§10`, `30 §5`–`§6.1`, `33 §9`, `37 §2`–`§5`).
+
+**Verdict: CLEAN.**
+
+---
+
+## Summary
+
+| Check | Verdict |
+|---|---|
+| C1 Write capability naming | DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED (9) |
+| C2 Model-authored policy operands | CLEAN |
+| C3 Authorised-loss quantities | CLEAN |
+| C4 CEO memory claim | DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED (2, both in diagrams) |
+| C5 Invariant citations resolve | DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED (1 residual range) |
+| C6 Registry counts | DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED (3 arithmetic) |
+| C7 Version headers and change records | DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED (1 artifact) |
+| C8 Removed broker | DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED (1) |
+| C9 Langfuse | CLEAN |
+| C10 Invariant-set and currency counts | CLEAN |
+| C11 symcc property set | DEFECTS FOUND AND FIXED (4) |
+| C12 Audit-unreachable behaviour | CLEAN |
+| C13 T-U1 status | CLEAN |
+| C14 New artifacts reachable | CLEAN |
+| C15 Structural integrity | CLEAN |
+
+**Seven checks clean, eight found defects, twenty-one individual fixes.** No residual accepted.
+
+**The pattern in what was found is worth recording.** Six of the eight failing checks were **counts, names or ranges that a document asserted and nobody recomputed** — the write capability's name in three diagrams, the invariant range in a residual-risk paragraph, three registry counts, the property set in four places. None was a design error. That is the same failure mode `47` identified as the dominant one in v1.0, reproduced by the remediation itself at smaller scale, and it is the argument for the registry being the single place any invariant number lives.
+
+**One process note for the second red team.** C6 found that the invariant registry disagreed with its own property table by six identifiers. **The registry is the artifact this package offers as the fix for v1.0's citation drift, and it shipped its first draft with an arithmetic error.** That is not a reason to distrust the mechanism — it is a reason to prefer mechanisms whose numbers are *derived* rather than *stated*, which is exactly what I7 does for authorised loss and what this pass had to do by hand for the registry.
